---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Memory" description="SIGRUN memory subsystem: buddy frame allocator, 4-level paging, the Mapper trait, heaps, and the per-address-space region list.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Subsystem 2 · memory</p>
  <h1>Memory: buddy frames, 4-level paging, heaps</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    SIGRUN owns physical memory end-to-end: a <strong>buddy allocator</strong> hands out power-of-two-sized
    frames, a 4-level <strong>page-table walker</strong> maps them into virtual address spaces, and a
    <strong>bump heap</strong> serves <code class="mono">alloc</code> before the scheduler exists.
  </p>

  <div class="pitch">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The three allocators</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Buddy</strong> (<code class="mono">memory/frame.rs</code>) — physical frames, power-of-two orders 0..20 (4&nbsp;KiB … 4&nbsp;GiB).</li>
        <li><strong>Bump</strong> (<code class="mono">main.rs</code>) — the global <code class="mono">#[global_allocator]</code>, 4&nbsp;MiB static array, never frees.</li>
        <li><strong>Slab</strong> (<code class="mono">memory/heap.rs</code>) — per-size caches 8…4096; compiled but not the live global allocator.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The two address worlds</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Physical: what <code class="mono">PhysAddr</code> means — real RAM/MMIO locations the frame allocator hands out.</li>
        <li>Virtual: what processes see. SIGRUN models it as an <code class="mono">AddressSpace</code> (own PML4 + a 32-entry <code class="mono">RegionList</code>) and a <code class="mono">Mapper</code> that edits page tables.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>1 · The buddy allocator</h2>
  <p>
    The buddy system keeps <strong>20 free lists</strong>, one per order. Order <em>n</em> holds blocks of
    <code class="mono">4&nbsp;KiB × 2ⁿ</code>. To allocate a 4&nbsp;KiB page you walk <em>up</em> the orders
    until a non-empty list is found, then <em>split</em> the block in half repeatedly, parking each freed
    buddy on the right list. Freeing is the reverse: if a block's buddy (its mirror-image neighbour, found
    with an XOR) is also free, the two coalesce back into the parent order.
  </p>

