  <div class="card">
    <h3>The alloc path in code</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c">// frame.rs — walk up until a non-empty list, then split down</span>
let mut actual_order = order;
while actual_order &lt;= MAX_ORDER &amp;&amp; self.free_lists[actual_order].is_empty() &#123;
    actual_order += 1;          <span class="c">// find the smallest order with a free block</span>
&#125;
let block = self.free_lists[actual_order].pop().unwrap();
while actual_order &gt; order &#123;     <span class="c">// split: park each leftover buddy one order down</span>
    actual_order -= 1;
    let buddy_addr = addr.as_u64() + (PAGE_SIZE &lt;&lt; actual_order);
    self.add_free_block(buddy_addr, actual_order);
&#125;
<span class="c">// deallocate: coalesce while the buddy (addr ^ block_size) is free</span>
fn buddy_address(&amp;self, addr: PhysAddr, order: usize) -&gt; PhysAddr &#123;
    PhysAddr::new(addr.as_u64() ^ (PAGE_SIZE &lt;&lt; order))
&#125;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>2 · 4-level paging and the Mapper</h2>
  <p>
    The virtual address splits into five fields: 9 bits per level (PML4 → PDPT → PD → PT) plus a 12-bit page
    offset. Each table is exactly one 4&nbsp;KiB frame holding 512 entries. <code class="mono">Mapper::map</code>
    walks down, lazily allocating &amp; zeroing any missing table from the frame allocator, then sets the leaf
    entry and flushes the TLB with <code class="mono">invlpg</code>.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-scroll">
