    <figcaption>
      The CPU does this walk in hardware on every memory access; SIGRUN mirrors it in software in
      <code class="mono">PageTableMapper</code> so it can create, unmap, and flag pages.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>The map path in code</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c">// mapper.rs — create-or-descend each level, then fill the leaf</span>
fn map(&amp;mut self, virt, frame, flags) -&gt; Result&lt;(), MemoryError&gt; &#123;
    let (pml4_idx, pdpt_idx, pd_idx, pt_idx) = page_table_indices(virt);
    let pdpt = self.get_or_create_pdpt(pml4_idx)?;   <span class="c">// alloc + clear a frame if absent</span>
    let pd   = self.get_or_create_pd(pdpt, pdpt_idx)?;
    let pt   = self.get_or_create_pt(pd, pd_idx)?;
    pt.entries[pt_idx].set(frame, flags | PRESENT);
    flush_tlb(virt);                    <span class="c">// invlpg — not the whole TLB</span>
    Ok(())
&#125;
<span class="c">// page_table.rs — the 9-bit field extractor</span>
pub const PML4_SHIFT: u64 = 39;  pub const PDPT_SHIFT: u64 = 30;
pub const PD_SHIFT: u64 = 21;    pub const PT_SHIFT: u64 = 12;
fn page_table_indices(addr) -&gt; (usize, usize, usize, usize) &#123;
    ((addr &gt;&gt; 39) &amp; 0o777, (addr &gt;&gt; 30) &amp; 0o777,
     (addr &gt;&gt; 21) &amp; 0o777, (addr &gt;&gt; 12) &amp; 0o777)
&#125;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>3 · The heap: why <code class="mono">static mut</code> and atomic bump</h2>
  <p>
    Rust's <code class="mono">alloc</code> needs a global allocator at compile time, but the kernel has no
    userspace memory manager yet — so SIGRUN declares a 4&nbsp;MiB array and bumps an atomic cursor through
    it. Two details matter:
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// main.rs — the global bump allocator</span>
#[repr(align(4096))]
struct StaticHeap([u8; HEAP_SIZE]);              <span class="c">// 4 MiB</span>
static mut HEAP_MEMORY: StaticHeap = StaticHeap([0; HEAP_SIZE]);
<span class="c">// 'static mut' is CRITICAL: it lands in .bss (zeroed, writable).</span>
<span class="c">// A plain 'static' would go to .rodata and the kernel would fault on write.</span>

unsafe impl GlobalAlloc for BumpAllocator &#123;
    unsafe fn alloc(&amp;self, layout: Layout) -&gt; *mut u8 &#123;
        loop &#123;                            <span class="c">// CAS loop: safe to share with the timer ISR</span>
            let cur = HEAP_NEXT.load(Relaxed);
            let aligned = (cur + layout.align() - 1) &amp; !(layout.align() - 1);
            let next = aligned + layout.size();
            if next &gt; HEAP_SIZE &#123; return null_mut(); &#125;
            if HEAP_NEXT.compare_exchange_weak(cur, next, SeqCst, Relaxed).is_ok() &#123;
                return (base + aligned) as *mut u8;
            &#125;
        &#125;
    &#125;
    unsafe fn dealloc(&amp;self, _ptr: *mut u8, _layout: Layout) &#123;&#125;  <span class="c">// never frees</span>
&#125;</code></pre>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="label">Why a CAS loop for a boot allocator?</span>
    Allocation must be safe even while interrupts are enabled — a timer tick could run kernel code that
    allocates. The atomic compare-exchange makes allocating lock-free; the cost is that freed memory is never
    reused, and the 4&nbsp;MiB pool is a hard ceiling (OOM returns <code class="mono">null</code>). A future
    step swaps in the slab allocator in <code class="mono">memory/heap.rs</code>, which already has
    per-size-class caches.
  </div>

  <h2>4 · Address spaces and the demo's U/S hack</h2>
  <p>
    <code class="mono">AddressSpace</code> owns a PML4 frame + a 32-slot <code class="mono">RegionList</code>
    (code/data/rodata/bss/stack/heap/vmalloc/mmap/…). Only the kernel space is really populated today. The
    ring-3 demo needs user-accessible memory, and rather than build a per-process page table yet, the kernel
    does the minimal thing — set the <strong>U/S bit</strong> on the boot page tables so ring 3 can touch all
    of the first 4&nbsp;GiB:
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// main.rs — enable_user_pages()</span>
let pml4 = addr_of!(_boot_pml4) as *mut u64;
let pdpt = addr_of!(_boot_pdpt) as *mut u64;
*pml4 |= 0x4;                     <span class="c">// U/S bit on PML4[0]</span>
for i in 0..4 &#123; *pdpt.add(i) |= 0x4; &#125;   <span class="c">// ... and all four 1 GiB entries</span>
<span class="c">// reload CR3 to flush the TLB</span>
asm!("mov &#123;tmp&#125;, cr3", "mov cr3, &#123;tmp&#125;", tmp = out(reg) _, ...);</code></pre>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">Interview honesty</span>
    This is explicitly a hack — everything in the first 4 GiB, including kernel code and the heap, becomes
    user-accessible. A real kernel would map only the user regions with U/S, keep kernel mappings supervisor-only
    (<code class="mono">SMEP</code>/<code class="mono">SMAP</code> are not even set), and validate syscall
    pointers. If asked "how would you harden this?", that's the answer to give.
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: memory</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why a buddy allocator?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          It gives power-of-two block sizes with cheap coalescing: freeing a block checks its buddy with one
          XOR and merges recursively, which keeps large contiguous regions available — critical for page
          tables and future big mappings. The cost is internal fragmentation (a 5&nbsp;KiB request rounds to
          8&nbsp;KiB) and O(log n) splitting; a slab or a general-purpose allocator handles small common sizes
          better, which is why a slab layer also exists.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "What are the 20 orders in MAX_ORDER?" — orders 0..20 = 4 KiB, 8 KiB, …, 4 GiB; the boot region (1 MiB..256 MiB) is seeded into the lists at init by repeatedly taking the largest aligned block. Note the frame allocator currently seeds a fixed 0x100000–0x11000000 window rather than parsing the multiboot memory map fully — one of the known limitations.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Walk me through mapping a page.</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Split the virtual address into four 9-bit indices. At each level, if the entry isn't present, allocate
          a zeroed table frame from the buddy allocator and record it. When you reach the PT, set the entry to
          <code class="mono">(frame | flags | PRESENT)</code> and invalidate that virtual address with
          <code class="mono">invlpg</code> so the CPU doesn't reuse a stale TLB entry. Unmapping retraces the
          path and clears the leaf; the tables themselves are intentionally not garbage-collected.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Why invlpg and not reload CR3?" — <code class="mono">flush_tlb</code> is lazy-correct: single-address invalidation is cheaper and doesn't evict other mappings. Reloading CR3 (<code class="mono">flush_tlb_all</code>) is only used when a whole table changes, e.g. the U/S hack.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why is the heap a <code class="mono">static mut</code> array and what breaks if it's a plain <code class="mono">static</code>?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Linker placement. <code class="mono">static mut</code> puts 4&nbsp;MiB of zeroed bytes in
          <code class="mono">.bss</code> — writable memory the loader zeroes for free. A plain
          <code class="mono">static</code> initializer would emit the array into <code class="mono">.rodata</code>
          (immutable pages), and the first <code class="mono">alloc</code> would page-fault. The
          <code class="mono">#[global_allocator]</code> also has to be <code class="mono">Sync</code> — SIGRUN
          marks the bump allocator <code class="mono">unsafe impl Sync</code> because the CAS cursor is the
          only shared state.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "What happens when the 4 MiB pool is exhausted?" — <code class="mono">alloc</code> returns null, and kernel allocation sites that unwrap (like the 8&nbsp;KiB task stacks) panic via <code class="mono">expect</code>. The pool is generous for the current workload, but this is the first thing a bigger system would outgrow.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>Where does the U/S bit hack sit, and why is it not a real isolation story?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          <code class="mono">enable_user_pages()</code> in <code class="mono">main.rs</code> sets U/S on the
          single boot PML4/PDPT — one address space shared by kernel and user. A real design keeps kernel
          mappings supervisor-only, builds a <em>separate</em> address space per process
          (<code class="mono">MemoryManager::alloc_address_space</code> already allocates a fresh PML4 for
          this), switches CR3 on context switch, and validates every user pointer in syscalls. The pieces to
          do it exist; wiring them into the scheduler/syscall path is future work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

